gAtoms -- bundled igraph C 1.0.1: ARPACK reverse-communication timing variables

Target:   src/igraph/vendor/lapack/dnaitr.c and dsaitr.c
Base:     official igraph 1.0.1 release sources, unmodified
Apply:    from the gAtoms package root,
          patch -p1 < tools/patches/igraph-1.0.1-arpack-timing-save.patch

Why:      Both routines return to the caller for a reverse-communication step
          (*ido = 1/2, goto L9000) in the middle of a timing interval: t2 is
          recorded with igrapharscnd_() before the return and used in
          'tmvopx += t3 - t2' after re-entry; the same holds for t0 and t4.
          The f2c translation left t0..t5 as plain automatic locals, so those
          reads see an indeterminate value -- undefined behaviour, and the
          -Wuninitialized diagnostics clang reports.  The original Fortran has
          a Save statement covering exactly these variables together with the
          accumulators.

Change:   t0, t2 and t4 get IGRAPH_F77_SAVE (static __thread), the same
          treatment every other cross-call local in these two files already
          has.  t1, t3 and t5 are always written and read within one call and
          stay automatic.  The values are timing only: they are accumulated
          into tmvopx/tmvbx/titref/tnaitr, which are never read anywhere, so no
          numerical result, no control flow and no Arnoldi iteration is
          affected.

--- a/vendor/lapack/dnaitr.c
+++ b/vendor/lapack/dnaitr.c
@@ -248,7 +248,18 @@
     /* Local variables */
     integer i__;
     IGRAPH_F77_SAVE integer j;
-    real t0, t1, t2, t3, t4, t5;
+    /* Only t0, t2 and t4 are read after the routine has returned to the caller
+     * for a reverse-communication step: t0 set at L396, first read at L523 with
+     * the return at L507 in between (and again at L892); t2 set at L562 and read
+     * at L583 across the return at L573, likewise L597->L623, L673->L698 and
+     * L776->L801; t4 set at L669 and read at L883 across the returns at L685 and
+     * L789.  They therefore need Fortran SAVE semantics, which IGRAPH_F77_SAVE
+     * provides -- the same treatment the other cross-call locals above already
+     * get.  t1, t3 and t5 are always written and read inside one call, so they
+     * stay automatic.  These are timing values only and have no effect on the
+     * Arnoldi iteration. */
+    IGRAPH_F77_SAVE real t0, t2, t4;
+    real t1, t3, t5;
     integer jj;
     IGRAPH_F77_SAVE integer ipj, irj;
     integer nbx=0;
--- a/vendor/lapack/dsaitr.c
+++ b/vendor/lapack/dsaitr.c
@@ -242,7 +242,18 @@
     /* Local variables */
     integer i__;
     IGRAPH_F77_SAVE integer j;
-    real t0, t1, t2, t3, t4, t5;
+    /* Only t0, t2 and t4 are read after the routine has returned to the caller
+     * for a reverse-communication step: t0 set at L371, first read at L508 with
+     * the return at L492 in between; t2 set at L547 and read at L567 across the
+     * return at L558, likewise L589->L614, L686->L711 and L788->L813; t4 set at
+     * L681 and read at L889 across the returns at L698 and L801.  They therefore
+     * need Fortran SAVE semantics, which IGRAPH_F77_SAVE provides -- the same
+     * treatment the other cross-call locals above already get.  t1, t3 and t5
+     * are always written and read inside one call, so they stay automatic.
+     * These are timing values only and have no effect on the Arnoldi
+     * iteration. */
+    IGRAPH_F77_SAVE real t0, t2, t4;
+    real t1, t3, t5;
     integer jj;
     IGRAPH_F77_SAVE integer ipj, irj;
     integer nbx=0;
